.TH CYAML 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cyaml \- overview of the YAML 1.2 parser, serializer and DOM
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
cyaml parses YAML text into a tree of nodes, which a program can read
and change, and then writes the tree back as YAML.  Each node has an
opaque cyaml handle.  cyaml_parse(3) describes the input that the
parser accepts and the limits that it applies; this page gives the
rules of the YAML language that are not specific to one function.
.fi
.SH "NODE TYPES"
.nf
cyaml_type(3) gives the type of a node, as a cyaml_node_type_t:

  CYAML_NULL         null, ~, or an empty value
  CYAML_BOOL         true or false, read with cyaml_bool_val(3)
  CYAML_INTEGER      a long long, read with cyaml_int_val(3)
  CYAML_FLOAT        a double, read with cyaml_double_val(3)
  CYAML_STRING       a UTF-8 string, read with cyaml_str_val(3)
  CYAML_LIST         a sequence of child nodes
  CYAML_DICTIONARY   a mapping from a string key to a child node

The keys of a dictionary are plain strings, not nodes.  When the
document writes a key as a number, a boolean, null or a collection, the
dictionary keeps that key as canonical text.  See cyaml_parse(3).
.fi
.SH "IMPLICIT TYPING"
.nf
The parser gives a type to a plain (unquoted, untagged) scalar with the
YAML 1.2 core schema:

  ~, null, Null, NULL, or empty          CYAML_NULL
  true, True, TRUE                       CYAML_BOOL (true)
  false, False, FALSE                    CYAML_BOOL (false)
  decimal integer, 0x hex, 0o octal      CYAML_INTEGER
  decimal float, exponent notation       CYAML_FLOAT
  .inf, -.inf, .nan (any case)           CYAML_FLOAT
  anything else                          CYAML_STRING

Under YAML 1.2, yes, no, on and off are strings.  An integer literal
that is outside the range of a long long becomes a CYAML_FLOAT, or a
CYAML_STRING if it is not a float either; it never wraps.  A
single-quoted or double-quoted scalar is always a string, whatever its
text.  A tag can override all these rules; see TAGS.
.fi
.SH "TAGS"
.nf
A tag attaches to its node.  It can be !!str, a custom !foo, a %TAG
shorthand, or a verbatim !<...>, and cyaml_node_tag(3) gives it back
in fully resolved form.  There are five scalar core-schema tags:
!!null, !!bool, !!int, !!float and !!str.  Each one forces its type on
a scalar of any style, replacing the type that the text alone gives;
for example, "!!str 42" is the string "42".  !!bool, !!int and !!float
examine the text of their scalar, and the parse fails if the text does
not match, while !!null accepts any text.  A custom tag forces no type,
so the node keeps its implicit type.

A tag on a dictionary key gives a type to the key in the same way, and
the dictionary keeps the canonical text of the typed key but not the
tag.  The serializers write a custom tag in its verbatim !<...> form,
and never in a %TAG shorthand.  They do not write a core-schema tag,
because implicit typing gives the same type again; see
cyaml_serialize(3).  cyaml_node_set_tag(3) and cyaml_set(3) keep a
core-schema tag only while it names the type of its node.
.fi
.SH "SUPPORTED YAML"
.nf
Block mappings, with implicit keys ("key: value") and explicit keys
("? key" then ": value"), which one mapping can mix in any sequence.
The ':' of an explicit value has this function only when a space, a
tab, a line end or the end of input comes after it, so ":name: value"
is an ordinary entry whose key is ":name".  An implicit key is on one
line, while an explicit key can continue on several lines.

Block sequences ("- item").  A sequence can be the value of a mapping
key, and it can then have the same indentation as that key.

Flow mappings ("{a: 1, b: 2}").  A bare key with no ':' gives a null
value, and so does a ':' with no value.  The parser accepts an
explicit "? key: value" entry, whose key can continue on several
lines.

Flow sequences ("[a, b, c]").  A "key: value" pair in a flow sequence
is a mapping with one entry, so "[foo: bar]" is equal to
"[{foo: bar}]".  The parser accepts an explicit "[? key: value]" entry.

Plain, single-quoted and double-quoted scalars, each of which can
continue on several lines: a line break becomes a space, and a blank
line becomes a newline.  A single-quoted scalar has no escapes, and in
it '' is one quote.  A double-quoted scalar accepts all YAML escapes,
and \euXXXX too.

Literal (|) and folded (>) block scalars, with the chomping indicators
|+ (keep), |- (strip) and | (clip, the default).

Anchors, aliases, tags and merge keys.  See below.

The %YAML directive, which can occur at most one time in each document
and which the parser examines against the MAJOR.MINOR grammar.

The %TAG directive, whose prefixes apply to the document that declares
them.

Documents with "---" and "..." markers, and streams of several
documents.  See cyaml_parse(3).

Comments, from '#' to the end of the line.

A cyaml string, anchor or tag cannot contain a null byte, because each
of them is a null-terminated C string, so encode such data, for
example as base64.  The parser does not decode a !!binary tag.
.fi
.SH "ANCHORS AND ALIASES"
.nf
"&name" on a node, a mapping key included, makes that node an anchor,
and "*name" later in the same document refers to that node.  The
parser replaces each alias with an independent deep copy of the
anchored node, so the tree has no shared nodes: a change to the copy
has no effect on the original, and a change to the original has no
effect on the copy.  An anchor applies only in its own document of a
stream.  A node can have at most one anchor and one tag, in either
sequence, and an alias can have no anchor and no tag.

Each copy that an alias or a merge key makes counts against a fixed
expansion limit, so a small document cannot expand into a tree of
unlimited size.  cyaml_parse(3) gives the numbers.
.fi
.SH "MERGE KEYS"
.nf
A mapping entry with the key "<<" merges other mappings into its own
mapping.  The key is a merge key in two cases:
  - It is a plain scalar with no tag.
  - It has the core merge tag, plain or quoted.  The core merge tag is
    !!merge, a %TAG shorthand of it, or the verbatim
    !<tag:yaml.org,2002:merge>.
A "<<" that is quoted and has no tag is an ordinary key, and so is a
"<<" with a different tag, such as !!str.  This rule applies in all
notations:
  - block mappings and flow mappings
  - implicit keys and explicit keys
  - the one-entry mapping of a "- <<: value" or "[<<: value]" sequence
    entry.

The value of a merge key is one mapping or a sequence of mappings,
usually aliases; a different value is a parse error.

The parser copies the members of each source into the mapping and then
removes the "<<" entry.  The precedence rules are:

  - A key that the mapping defines itself always keeps its own value,
    whether it comes before or after the "<<" entry.
  - For a sequence of sources, an earlier source has priority over a
    later source.

The merged members go in the place of the "<<" entry in the member
order of the mapping, after the explicit members before "<<" and
before the explicit members after "<<".  The parser adds them source
by source, each source in its own member order.  For example:

    defaults: &d {timeout: 30, retries: 3}
    service:
      <<: *d
      timeout: 60

gives service the members retries: 3 and then timeout: 60.
.fi
.SH "DUPLICATE KEYS"
.nf
The parser accepts a key that occurs more than one time in a mapping:
the last value becomes the value of the key, and the key keeps the
place of its first occurrence.  The parser frees the earlier value
without reporting it.  Common YAML parsers do the same, but YAML 1.2
does not require it, so a program must not expect that a YAML parser
finds a duplicate key.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP"
.nf
cyaml_parse(3), cyaml_clone(3) and the cyaml_create_*(3) functions
give a tree that the caller owns and frees with cyaml_destroy(3).
cyaml_get(3), cyaml_list_get(3), cyaml_dictionary_get(3) and the
dictionary cursor give borrowed references, and the tree keeps the
ownership of these nodes.  cyaml_list_push(3) and
cyaml_dictionary_set(3) take the child; their pages give the one
return code that leaves the child with the caller.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A tree is not thread safe, but several threads can read one tree at
the same time while no thread changes it.  Separate trees are
independent, so separate threads can parse and serialize at the same
time.
.fi
